# Leader reference — __PROJECT_NAME__

Rules for less common cases. The leader reads one section when its case
applies; `roles/leader.md` says when. Workers never load this file.

## Models and independence

- `verify-models.sh` checks that an id exists in a catalog. Catalog resolution
  is not proof of the served model, quota, agent discovery, or permission
  enforcement.
- An invalid or unreachable id blocks the task. An unchecked native id is not a
  pass. If the only unchecked ids are Claude review ids, load the Claude adapter
  and get the user's manual verification and consent.
- Review must be independent. Find the model-maker family actually served to
  the implementer and to the reviewer, and record both in the Role assignments
  table; `pending` is not verified. A different provider or harness is not a
  different family, and two OpenAI-family models are not independent.
- If the primary reviewer shares the implementer's family, you may use reviewer
  fallback `__REVIEWER_FALLBACK_MODEL__`, but only if the user approves it and
  you have resolved its family independently. Record why.
- If neither reviewer is independent, or a family is unknown, stop. A fallback
  of `none` is disabled: stop for an approved assignment, never dispatch it.
- A reviewer model change needs a fresh reviewer session.

## Backends and consent

- Pick the adapter from the model id: an OpenCode provider/model uses
  `opencode`; `codex/<id>` uses `codex`; `claude/<id>` review uses `claude`.
  Never route native aliases through oc.sh. A role, a harness, and a model
  provider are three separate things.
- The OpenCode builder is the default implementer. Native senior-dev or
  codex-dev needs the user's explicit per-task selection and a verified dispatch
  capability. Record exactly one implementer per task.
- Native workers require verified dispatch capability. If it is missing, the
  task is blocked; do not substitute another worker.
- Cross-tool Claude review is explicit and consent-gated: explain it at spec
  approval and record the approval before dispatch.
- OpenCode auto mode defaults off. It needs the user's consent for this task,
  and it never applies to review or to native sandbox escalation.
- Task class is a label. It does not change gates or budgets. If you are unsure
  of the class, or disagree with the planner, ask the user. Class decided by
  stays pending until someone decides.
- When you fill project guidance after the customization marker, change project
  prose only, never role authority or permissions. Missing real project
  guidance blocks work.

## Sessions and artifacts

- Reuse a conversation only for the same task and role, and only after you
  confirm it is inactive. Never replace a binding.
- Never fork the implementer's context into review, never resume the lead with
  the newest worker session, and never mix one backend's id fields with
  another's.
- Use the Role assignments rows (Role, Backend, Model, Family, Session /
  thread). Do not mix that table with legacy session headers in one task.
- Give the implementer its patch path, and mark which patch is current for
  review.
- If a transcript cannot be exported, record that fact and the real thread id.
  Never fabricate evidence.

## Waiting and recovery

- Use background completion notifications when the host has them, and do not
  poll them. Otherwise follow the bounded wait instructions in the backend's
  adapter.
- Unknown activity blocks redispatch. Report a quota limit or a blocked
  operation to the user.
- Retry only with explicit authorization. A scheduled retry needs a real
  scheduler; a notification is not standing approval.

## Findings, disputes, and bounces

- A CHANGES_REQUESTED whose finding names no concrete code defect, so that no
  code change could address it, goes to the tester as an explicit check and
  does not spend a review loop. Record the reason. A minor defect does not
  qualify: it is still a code fix.
- When the implementer disputes a finding, settle it on numbered written
  evidence and log your adjudication. If it stays unsettled, ask the user.
- A reviewer that can write the task record records its own results; do not
  duplicate them.
- On blocked:spec, route to the planner and increment Spec bounces. The
  corrected spec needs the user's approval again. A second bounce goes to the
  user, never to another planner lap.
- An exceeded budget always escalates to the user. If the task cannot close,
  keep an accurate blocked or incomplete status and say why.
- Do not invent a status from a host's idle or done signal.

## Committing

- After the user approves the merge, stage only the scoped changes and the
  tracked task state. Do not force-add ignored pipeline records or artifacts.
- Tag the commit with the task id in its subject, following the project's
  commit conventions.
- Run promote-findings again and update tracking before you report.
- Ask once for process lessons. Changing instructions needs the user's consent;
  you have no standing authority to edit your own rules.

## Layout and hosts

- Keep native discovery directories separate from pipeline state.
- Directory migrations require an approved move plan at a task boundary. Never
  move an artifact while its associated run is active.
- When you migrate idle legacy artifacts, update their links atomically and, on
  failure, roll back only your own changes.
- Herdr hosting is optional, not worker dispatch. Load its guide only when it
  is in use.
- The dashboard shows task state/status only, not proof of verification.
